# Frontmatter schema — fillable template

> A frontmatter schema defines the structured metadata that every note has. Use this template to design your schema. **The friend running Alice's methodology fills in this template for their own vault, not the operator's specific schema.**

---

# Frontmatter schema — [vault name]

## Required fields (every note has these)

| Field | Type | Format | Source | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `id` | string | 7-char alphanumeric hash | generator | unique identifier |
| `created` | ISO8601-UTC | YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ | writer | when first written |
| `updated` | ISO8601-UTC | YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ | editor | when last edited |
| `type` | enum | atomic / literature / inbox / moc / decision / archive / citation / ... | writer | what kind of note |
| `status` | enum | draft / active / stale / archived | writer | lifecycle stage |
| `source` | string | who/where wrote this | writer | provenance |

## Optional fields (some notes have these)

| Field | Type | Format | Source | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `tags` | array | `<namespace>:<value>, ...` | writer | namespaced filter |
| `confidence` | float | 0.0-1.0 | writer | operator's confidence |
| `links` | array | `<note-id>, ...` | writer | wikilinks to related notes |

## Tag namespaces (closed list)

| Namespace | Purpose | Example values |
|---|---|---|
| `domain:` | topical area | `domain:<area>` |
| `project:` | project the note belongs to | `project:<slug>` |
| `kind:` | kind of note | `kind:spec`, `kind:rule`, `kind:incident` |
| `source:` | where the note came from | `source:operator`, `source:x-post` |
| `private:` | privacy marker | `private:operator`, `private:redact` |

## Field validation rules

- **Date format:** ISO8601-UTC (`YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ`)
- **Tag values:** lowercase, namespaced (`domain:foo`, not `Domain: Foo`)
- **Confidence:** 0.0-1.0 (not "high" / "medium" / "low")
- **Note IDs:** 7-char alphanumeric hash, unique per note
- **Wikilinks:** `[[note-id]]` for internal; `[text](path)` for external

## Field lifecycle (when to revise)

| Trigger | Action |
|---|---|
| A new field is queryable | Add the field; migrate existing notes |
| An existing field is unused | Mark as deprecated; remove after migration |
| A field's source is unclear | Resolve source or remove field |
| Schema crosses 15 fields | Audit and consolidate |

---

## The 5-question schema audit (run quarterly)

1. **Is each field queryable?** (Operator or agent can filter / sort / group by it.)
2. **Is each field stable?** (The value doesn't change every edit.)
3. **Is each field's source clear?** (Operator or agent knows who fills it.)
4. **Is each field actually used?** (At least 50% of notes have it set.)
5. **Is each field in the right tier?** (See `methodology/01a-decide-memory.md`.)

---

## Approval

- [ ] Required fields are listed with type, format, source, purpose
- [ ] Optional fields are listed with type, format, source, purpose
- [ ] Tag namespaces are a closed list with example values
- [ ] Field validation rules are documented
- [ ] Field lifecycle triggers are documented
- [ ] The 5-question schema audit is scheduled quarterly
- [ ] The schema fits in 1-2 pages (longer = needs audit)

Once all 7 boxes are checked, the frontmatter schema is ready. Use it to design new notes and migrate existing notes.

---

## How the friend uses this template

1. **List your required fields.** Start with the Alice minimum (id, created, updated, type, status, source).
2. **Add optional fields.** Add a field only when it's queryable, stable, and has a clear source.
3. **Define your tag namespaces.** Start with the Alice minimum (domain, project, kind, source, private).
4. **Document validation rules.** Date formats, tag conventions, ID format.
5. **Schedule the quarterly schema audit.** Use the 5-question audit.
6. **Migrate existing notes.** When the schema changes, update all existing notes.

The frontmatter schema is **operator-context.** The friend running Alice's methodology designs their own schema for their own context. They do NOT copy the operator's specific fields; they apply the method to derive their own.